# Universal Cover of $\mathbb{R}P^2 \setminus \{p\}$

**Answer check:** The problem statement contains no leaked answer, solution sketch, or formalization notes. Proceeding.

---

## Claim

The universal cover of $\mathbb{R}P^2 \setminus \{p\}$ is $\mathbb{R}^2$.

---

## Proof

### Step 1. Cell decomposition and deformation retraction

Equip $\mathbb{R}P^2$ with its standard CW structure: one $0$-cell, one $1$-cell, and one $2$-cell. The $1$-skeleton is a circle $S^1$, and the $2$-cell $D^2$ is attached via the map $f\colon \partial D^2 \to S^1$ of degree $2$.

Since $\mathbb{R}P^2$ is a connected manifold, it is homogeneous: any point can be carried to any other by a homeomorphism. Thus we may assume the removed point $p$ lies in the **interior** of the $2$-cell.

The open $2$-cell with a point removed, $D^2 \setminus \{p\}$, deformation retracts onto $\partial D^2 = S^1$. This retraction takes place entirely in the interior of the $2$-cell, so it is compatible with the quotient defining $\mathbb{R}P^2$. Composing with the attaching map $f$, we obtain a deformation retraction

$$\mathbb{R}P^2 \setminus \{p\} \;\simeq\; S^1 \quad\text{(the $1$-skeleton)}.$$

Hence $\pi_1(\mathbb{R}P^2 \setminus \{p\}) \cong \mathbb{Z}$.

### Step 2. Identifying the surface

The space $\mathbb{R}P^2 \setminus \{p\}$ is a non-compact surface. It is **non-orientable**: $\mathbb{R}P^2$ contains a Möbius band (a neighborhood of the $1$-skeleton), and removing a point from the interior of the $2$-cell does not destroy it. By the classification of non-compact surfaces, the unique non-orientable, non-compact surface with $\pi_1 \cong \mathbb{Z}$ is the **open Möbius band**. Thus

$$\mathbb{R}P^2 \setminus \{p\} \;\cong\; \text{open Möbius band}.$$

### Step 3. The universal cover is $\mathbb{R}^2$

A non-compact surface has a simply connected, non-compact universal cover. By the classification of simply connected surfaces, the only non-compact one is $\mathbb{R}^2$ (since $S^2$ is compact, and the universal cover must be non-compact because $\pi_1 \cong \mathbb{Z}$ gives infinite fibers). Therefore the universal cover is $\mathbb{R}^2$.

### Step 4. Verification via the $S^2$ model

Write $\mathbb{R}P^2 = S^2 / \mathbb{Z}_2$ where $\mathbb{Z}_2$ acts by the antipodal map. Removing $[x] \in \mathbb{R}P^2$ removes the orbit $\{x, -x\} \subset S^2$:

$$\mathbb{R}P^2 \setminus \{[x]\} \;=\; \bigl(S^2 \setminus \{x, -x\}\bigr) / \mathbb{Z}_2.$$

Choosing $x$ and $-x$ as the north and south poles, $S^2 \setminus \{N, S\} \cong S^1 \times \mathbb{R}$ (an open cylinder). In coordinates $(\theta, t)$ with $\theta \in \mathbb{R}/2\pi\mathbb{Z}$ and $t \in \mathbb{R}$ (height), the antipodal action is

$$\sigma_0\colon (\theta, t) \;\longmapsto\; (\theta + \pi,\; -t).$$

The universal cover of $S^1 \times \mathbb{R}$ is $\mathbb{R}^2$ with coordinates $(\phi, t)$, deck transformation $T\colon (\phi,t) \mapsto (\phi + 2\pi, t)$. The map $\sigma_0$ lifts to

$$\sigma\colon (\phi, t) \;\longmapsto\; (\phi + \pi,\; -t),$$

which is a **glide reflection** on $\mathbb{R}^2$. Since $\sigma^2 = T$, the full group acting on $\mathbb{R}^2$ that produces $\mathbb{R}P^2 \setminus \{p\}$ is

$$\langle \sigma \rangle \;\cong\; \mathbb{Z}, \qquad \sigma\colon (\phi,t) \mapsto (\phi+\pi, -t).$$

This action is free and properly discontinuous (the $\phi$-component always translates by $\pi$), so

$$\mathbb{R}P^2 \setminus \{p\} \;=\; \mathbb{R}^2 / \langle \sigma \rangle,$$

confirming that **$\mathbb{R}^2$ is the universal cover** with deck transformation group $\mathbb{Z}$ generated by the glide reflection $\sigma$. The quotient of $\mathbb{R}^2$ by a glide reflection is indeed an open Möbius band, consistent with Step 2.

---

**Conclusion.** The universal cover of $\mathbb{R}P^2$ with a point removed is $\mathbb{R}^2$, with deck group $\mathbb{Z}$ acting by the glide reflection $(\phi, t) \mapsto (\phi + \pi, -t)$.

